## Conventions

- **Layout.** `src/app.ts` builds the Fastify app (routes, hooks) without listening; `src/server.ts` starts it. Add routes as Fastify plugins under `src/routes/` registered in `buildApp`. ESM throughout: relative imports end in `.js`.
- **TypeScript strict.** No `any` without a justifying comment; `devbox run typecheck` must be clean.
- **Validation:** declare a JSON schema (`schema: { body, querystring, params, response }`) on every route that takes input, so that Fastify rejects bad input with 400 before your handler runs.
- **Config:** read exclusively from environment variables in `src/config.ts` (document each one in `docs/env-vars.md`).
- **Probes:** `/health` (liveness) and `/ready` (readiness) must keep responding; add a check to `ready` in `buildApp` for every dependency the service needs.
- **Logging:** log through the request's logger (`request.log.info({ orderId }, "order created")`) or the app's (`app.log`): one JSON line each{% if needs_observability %}, carrying `trace_id` and `span_id` inside an active span{% endif %}. Never log secrets or whole request bodies.
{%- if needs_observability %}
- **Observability:** `/metrics` (Prometheus, `src/metrics.ts`: default Node metrics, `http_requests_total`, `http_request_duration_seconds`); OpenTelemetry tracing in `src/telemetry.ts`, preloaded with `node --import`, exporting only when `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` is set.
{%- endif %}
{%- if needs_database %}
- **Database:** PostgreSQL through Drizzle ORM (`src/db/`). The schema is `src/db/schema.ts`; every change gets a SQL migration from `devbox run migrate-new <name>` in `drizzle/` (commit it; never edit a released migration). The service applies pending migrations at startup. Connection settings are the `PG*` variables the platform's postgres resource injects (`docs/env-vars.md`); locally `devbox run db-up` starts a matching PostgreSQL. Integration tests use Testcontainers (`tests/db.test.ts`, Docker required). Readiness includes the database.
{%- endif %}
- **Tests:** Vitest in `tests/` (`*.test.ts`); call the app with `app.inject(...)` instead of opening a port. Smoke tests live in `tests/smoke/` (see Smoke tests).
- **Docs:** feature specs in `docs/specs/` (index in `docs/backlog.md`), ADRs in `docs/adr/`.

## Smoke tests

`devbox run smoke` runs the smoke tests against a deployed instance (`SMOKE_BASE_URL`); the platform runs it after every deploy to dev and staging and on `/instance:verify <env>`. It also sets `SMOKE_ENV` (`dev`, `staging`, `prod`) and, outside dev, `SMOKE_READ_ONLY=1`: then tests named `@writes` are not run, so staging and prod never get smoke data.

- A smoke test is a Vitest test in `tests/smoke/` with `@smoke` in its name, inside `describe.skipIf(!process.env.SMOKE_BASE_URL)` (`tests/smoke/health.test.ts`).
- A smoke test reaches the service only through the base URL (`fetch(new URL(path, base))`), creates its own uniquely named data (`smoke-<uuid>`) and never changes or deletes data it did not create.
- Mark every smoke test that creates, changes or deletes data as writing: `@writes` next to `@smoke` in its name. It runs only in dev.
- Keep at least one read-only smoke check (reads, health, lists) for every behaviour you want checked in staging and prod. Outside dev, a run in which no smoke test passed is reported as **not run** (no read-only smoke test), never as passed.

## Docker image pipeline

CI's `docker` job builds the multi-stage, non-root image on every run and pushes on `main` (`latest`, `sha-<40>`, `sha-<short>`); a `v*.*.*` tag gives the tested `sha-<40>` image its semver tags (`release-image`). Registry: `{{ docker_registry }}/{{ project_name }}`, authenticated with `GITHUB_TOKEN`.

A private `@<owner>` package needs the library's `.npmrc` lines and the repository secret `PACKAGES_READ_TOKEN`: CI passes it to the install steps and to the image build as a BuildKit secret (never in a layer or build argument). Locally, `export NODE_AUTH_TOKEN=$(gh auth token)` before `devbox run image-build`; without it the build runs with no token. <!-- pragma: allowlist secret -->

## Deployment

This repo ships **only a container image**. Kubernetes manifests, environment wiring (e.g. `API_URL`), replicas, resources and exposure belong to the system's `gitops-app` repo (platform ADR-017): register the component with `/component:add {{ project_name }}` in the system's repo (needs the GitHub topic `deployable-service`, set by `/component:new`), then `/instance:promote {{ project_name }} --to <env>`. Change how the service runs by editing its entry under `components:` in that repo's `system.yaml`; change the port, probe paths or user in the Dockerfile/app and update the entry to match.

